{"reference":"1 Corinthians 13","book":"1 Corinthians","chapter":13,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"Reading the Chapter in Its Setting","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"Chapter 13 stands between discussions of spiritual gifts. Its focus is not primarily romantic sentiment: it addresses rivalry, impressive speech, knowledge, and self-display by making patient, non-self-seeking conduct the measure of gifts and sacrifice.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 12:31"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_013_b25a5690f526c0acecab8bb2"]},{"id":"block_2","text":"The descriptions of patience, kindness, truth, and refusal to seek one’s own advantage give love a communal shape. They make noncoercion and truthfulness relevant to correction, leadership, reconciliation, and the use of gifts.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 12:31","1 Corinthians 14:1"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_013_6e525455b4925348015d3a1e"]},{"id":"block_3","text":"Acts portrays recognizable diaspora languages at Pentecost, but how that phenomenon relates to the tongues discussed in Corinthian worship remains debated. That comparison should not settle by itself what “tongues” means in this chapter.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 12:10-31","1 Corinthians 14:1-40"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_013_2655ca16bdbd4436f8fe3057"]}]},{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Questions About Endurance and Completion","blocks":[{"id":"block_4","text":"“Bears” and “endures” describe love’s steadfastness, but they cannot require a victim to tolerate abuse, conceal harm, or remain unsafe. The surrounding qualities—especially love’s joy in truth rather than wrongdoing—also resist using this passage to excuse harm.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 13:1","1 Corinthians 13:1-13","1 Corinthians 13:4-7"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_013_e7572bc6e8b4e7ec404178e7"]},{"id":"block_5","text":"Verses 8–13 contrast prophecy, tongues, and knowledge with love’s endurance, using images of partial knowledge, maturity, and indirect sight. Readers disagree about what “the perfect” means and about whether these verses settle later debates over the continuation or cessation of particular gifts. The chapter itself establishes the contrast, but does not provide enough detail to resolve all those later questions with certainty.","verse_refs":["1 Corinthians 13:1","1 Corinthians 13:8-13"],"provenance_refs":["reader_path_1_corinthians_013_59765044b2887330ddb4ac3d","reader_path_1_corinthians_013_07ed69c3965568d3ada93a91"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}